---
title: MLE vs. MAP vs. Full Posterior for ODE Parameter Estimation
author: Arnav Kapoor
---

# Point estimates and posteriors: MLE, MAP, and full MCMC compared

This benchmark addresses part of [SciMLBenchmarks.jl#28](https://github.com/SciML/SciMLBenchmarks.jl/issues/28):
comparing three ways of using the same probabilistic model of noisy ODE observations to
answer "what are the parameters?" -- maximum likelihood estimation (MLE, a point estimate
that ignores priors entirely), maximum a posteriori estimation (MAP, a point estimate that
incorporates priors), and full Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) sampling of the posterior
distribution (which additionally quantifies *uncertainty* in each parameter, not just a
single best value). All three are computed here from the *same* `Turing.jl` model applied
to the same noisy Lotka-Volterra dataset used by this repository's other parameter
estimation and [DiffEqBayes](DiffEqBayesLotkaVolterra.html) pages, so the only thing that
differs between them is how that one model is queried.

```julia
using Turing, OrdinaryDiffEq, Distributions, LinearAlgebra
using ParameterizedFunctions, BenchmarkTools, Plots, Random, Statistics, DataFrames
gr(fmt = :png)
```

## Model and synthetic data

```julia
f = @ode_def LotkaVolterraTest begin
    dx = a*x - b*x*y
    dy = -c*y + d*x*y
end a b c d

u0 = [1.0, 1.0]
p_true = [1.5, 1.0, 3.0, 1.0]
tspan = (0.0, 3.0)
prob = ODEProblem(f, u0, tspan, p_true)

tgrid = collect(0.0:0.1:3.0)
sol_true = solve(prob, Tsit5(), saveat = tgrid, reltol = 1e-9, abstol = 1e-9)
data_clean = Array(sol_true)

Random.seed!(1)
sigma_true = 0.1
data_noisy = data_clean .+ sigma_true .* randn(size(data_clean))

plot(tgrid, data_clean', label = ["x (truth)" "y (truth)"], xlabel = "time",
    title = "Synthetic Lotka-Volterra data", legend = :outertopright)
scatter!(tgrid, data_noisy', label = ["x (observed)" "y (observed)"], markersize = 3)
```

## The probabilistic model

A single `Turing.jl` model with weakly-informative priors on the four kinetic parameters
and the (also unknown) observation noise level `sigma`; the likelihood is a multivariate
Normal around the ODE solution at each observed time, exactly as in the `DiffEqBayes`-based
pages in this folder.

```julia
@model function lv_model(data, prob)
    a ~ truncated(Normal(1.5, 0.5); lower = 0)
    b ~ truncated(Normal(1.0, 0.5); lower = 0)
    c ~ truncated(Normal(3.0, 0.5); lower = 0)
    d ~ truncated(Normal(1.0, 0.5); lower = 0)
    sigma ~ truncated(Normal(0.1, 0.1); lower = 0)

    p = [a, b, c, d]
    _prob = remake(prob, p = p)
    predicted = solve(_prob, Tsit5(), saveat = tgrid, reltol = 1e-8, abstol = 1e-8)
    if predicted.retcode !== ReturnCode.Success
        Turing.@addlogprob! -Inf
        return
    end
    for i in eachindex(predicted.t)
        data[:, i] ~ MvNormal(collect(predicted.u[i]), sigma^2 * I)
    end
end

model = lv_model(data_noisy, prob)
```

## Maximum likelihood estimation (MLE)

Ignores the priors entirely and finds the single parameter vector that maximizes the
likelihood of the observed data -- the classical frequentist point estimate, equivalent to
the `build_loss_objective`-based fits used elsewhere in this repository's
[ParameterEstimation](../ParameterEstimation) pages, but computed here through `Turing.jl`'s
own optimizer interface against the same model used for MAP and MCMC below.

```julia
mle_result = @btime maximum_likelihood($model)
mle_result.params
```

## Maximum a posteriori estimation (MAP)

Uses the same optimizer, but maximizes the *posterior* density (likelihood times priors)
instead of the likelihood alone -- still a single point estimate, but one that is
regularized toward the prior whenever the data are not fully informative about a parameter.

```julia
map_result = @btime maximum_a_posteriori($model)
map_result.params
```

## Full posterior via MCMC

Samples the entire posterior distribution with the No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS), giving not
just a point estimate but a full characterization of parameter uncertainty (and of any
correlations between parameters) at substantially higher computational cost than either
point-estimation method above.

```julia
chain = @btime sample($model, NUTS(0.65), 1000)
```

```julia
posterior_means = Dict(sym => mean(vec(chain[sym])) for sym in [:a, :b, :c, :d, :sigma])
posterior_stds = Dict(sym => std(vec(chain[sym])) for sym in [:a, :b, :c, :d, :sigma])
posterior_means, posterior_stds
```

## Comparison

```julia
syms = [:a, :b, :c, :d, :sigma]
df = DataFrame(
    parameter = string.(syms),
    truth = [p_true; sigma_true],
    mle = [mle_result.params[s] for s in syms],
    map = [map_result.params[s] for s in syms],
    posterior_mean = [posterior_means[s] for s in syms],
    posterior_std = [posterior_stds[s] for s in syms])
df
```

MLE and MAP agree closely with each other and with the posterior mean here, since the
priors above are only weakly informative relative to how much this dataset constrains the
parameters; MAP would pull noticeably harder toward the prior mean with either a smaller
dataset or tighter data-implied likelihood contours. The posterior standard deviations --
available from MCMC but not from either point estimate -- show that `c` (the predator death
rate) is markedly less well-constrained by this dataset than `a`, `b`, or `d`, information
neither MLE nor MAP can surface on their own.

```julia
trace_plots = [plot(vec(chain[s]), xlabel = "iteration", ylabel = string(s), legend = false,
    title = string(s)) for s in syms]
plot(trace_plots..., layout = (3, 2), size = (900, 700))
```

# Conclusion

MLE and MAP both return a single "best" parameter vector at a fraction of full MCMC's
cost, and agree closely with each other whenever the data are informative enough that the
priors barely matter -- which was true here. Choose between them based on whether you trust
your priors to add real information (favoring MAP, especially with limited data) or would
rather let the data speak entirely for itself (favoring MLE). Neither, however, can tell you
*how much* to trust the point estimate they return: only the full posterior does that,
revealing here that `c` carries substantially more uncertainty than the other three
parameters despite all four converging to similar-looking point estimates. That extra
information costs real compute -- the NUTS chain above takes far longer than either
optimization-based estimate -- which is the central trade-off this benchmark is meant to
make concrete: point estimation for speed, full posterior sampling when the shape and
width of the uncertainty matters as much as its center.

